<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Round-truncate Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A small business recorded its monthly revenue as exactly  \({@val@}\) Ksh. The accountant needs to report this figure to the nearest thousand shillings.</p>

<p>Two accountants report the value differently:</p>
<p><strong>Accountant A</strong> rounds the value to the nearest thousand.</p>
<p><strong>Accountant B</strong> truncates the value to the nearest thousand (simply drops the extra digits, without rounding).</p>

<p><strong>Rounding (Accountant A)</strong></p>

<p>(a) Find the absolute error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the relative error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Find the percentage error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p><strong>Truncation (Accountant B)</strong></p>

<p>(d) Find the absolute error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>(e) Find the relative error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p>(f) Find the percentage error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3> Worked Solution.</h3>
<p>The business recorded its exact monthly revenue as \({@val@}\) Ksh.</p>

<p><strong>Rounding (Accountant A):</strong></p>
<p>Rounding \({@val@}\) to the nearest thousand gives \({@rounded@}\) Ksh.</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Absolute error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = |\text{rounded value} - \text{working value}| = |{@rounded@} - {@val@}| = {@ta1@} \text{ Ksh} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{working value}} = \frac{{@ta1@}}{{@val@}} \approx {@ta2@} \]</p>

<p>(c) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta2@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta3@}\% \]</p>

<p><strong>Truncation (Accountant B):</strong></p>
<p>Truncating \({@val@}) to the nearest thousand (dropping the extra digits without rounding) gives \({@truncated@}\) Ksh.</p>

<p>(d) <strong>Absolute error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = |\text{truncated value} - \text{working value}| = |{@truncated@} - {@val@}| = {@ta4@} \text{ Ksh} \]</p>

<p>(e) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{working value}} = \frac{{@ta4@}}{{@val@}} \approx {@ta5@} \]</p>

<p>(f) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta5@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta6@}\% \]</p>

<p><strong>Comparing the two methods:</strong> Truncation always rounds down, so it either matches rounding (when the amount being dropped is small) or produces a bigger error than rounding, since rounding can adjust the value up or down to whichever is closer. In business reporting, this means truncation can sometimes understate revenue more than proper rounding would.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
